Recall that a subset \( S \subset  {\mathbf{R}}^{3} \) is a regular surface if, for every point \( p \in  S \) , there exist a neighborhood \( V \) of \( p \) in \( {\mathbf{R}}^{3} \) and a mapping \( \mathbf{x} : U \subset  {\mathbf{R}}^{2} \rightarrow  V \cap  S \) of an open set \( U \subset  {\mathbf{R}}^{2} \) onto \( V \cap  S \) , such that:

(a) \( x \) is a differentiable homeomorphism;

(b) The differential \( {\left( dx\right) }_{q} : {\mathbf{R}}^{2} \rightarrow  {\mathbf{R}}^{3} \) is injective for all \( q \in  U \) (See M. do Carmo, [dC 2], Chap. 2).

