Lemma 9.7.2 (Convexity). Considering eigenvalues \( {\lambda }_{1} \leq  {\lambda }_{2} \leq  {\lambda }_{3} \) as functions on the vector space of symmetric endomorphisms of \( {\mathbb{R}}^{3} \) , we have

(i) \( {\lambda }_{1} + {\lambda }_{2} + {\lambda }_{3} \) is linear (convex and concave);

(ii) \( {\lambda }_{1} \) is concave.

As a consequence, we have that

(a) \( {\lambda }_{3} \) is convex;

(b) \( {\lambda }_{1} + {\lambda }_{2} \) is concave;

(c) \( {\lambda }_{3} - {\lambda }_{1} \) is convex.

Moreover, if \( \alpha  : \mathcal{X} \rightarrow  \mathbb{R} \) is a concave function on a vector space \( \mathcal{X} \) , and \( \beta  : \lbrack 0,\infty ) \rightarrow  \mathbb{R} \) is concave and increasing, then \( \beta  \circ  \alpha  : {\alpha }^{-1}\left( {\lbrack 0,\infty }\right) ) \subset  \mathcal{X} \rightarrow  \mathbb{R} \) is concave.

