DEFINITION 11.50 (Pinching family of convex cones). We call a continuous family \( C\left( s\right)  \subset  {S}_{B}^{2}\left( {\mathfrak{{so}}\left( n\right) }\right) , s \in  \lbrack 0,1) \) , of top-dimensional closed convex cones a pinching family (with respect to the ODE \( \frac{d}{dt}\mathbf{R} = {\mathbf{R}}^{2} + {\mathbf{R}}^{\# } \) ) if

(1) \( C\left( s\right) \) is \( \mathrm{O}\left( n\right) \) -invariant for every \( s \in  \lbrack 0,1) \) ;

(2) each \( \mathbf{R} \in  C\left( s\right)  \smallsetminus  \{ 0\} \) has positive scalar curvature;

(3) \( {\mathbf{R}}^{2} + {\mathbf{R}}^{\# } \) lies in the interior of the tangent cone of \( C\left( s\right) \) at \( \mathbf{R} \) for all \( \mathbf{R} \in  C\left( s\right)  \smallsetminus  \{ 0\} \) and \( s \in  \left( {0,1}\right) \) ;

(4) the cones \( C\left( s\right) \) converge in the pointed Hausdorff topology to the 1-dimensional cone \( {\mathbb{R}}_{ + }\mathbf{I} \) as \( s \rightarrow  1 \) (where the base points are the origin). \( {}^{25} \)

Abstracting this construction in higher dimensions, Böhm and Wilking prove the existence of a continuous 1-parameter family of \( \mathrm{O}\left( n\right) \) -invariant closed convex cones \( \{ C\left( s\right) {\} }_{s \in  \lbrack 0,1)} \) , called a ’pinching family’, with the following properties:

(1) except for 0 , their elements have positive scalar curvature;

(2) the ODE \( \frac{d}{dt}\mathbf{R} = Q\left( \mathbf{R}\right) \) preserves the cones;

(3) the cones limit to \( {\mathbb{R}}_{ + }\mathbf{I} \) as \( s \rightarrow  1 \) ;

(4) \( C\left( 0\right) \) is the cone of 2-nonnegative algebraic curvature operators.

