REMARK 15.39 (Interpretation as space-time splitting theorem). As we shall see in Chapter 22 of Part III of this volume, the steady gradient soliton equation \( {R}_{ij} + {
abla }_{i}{
abla }_{j}f = 0 \) , roughly speaking, says that the space-time vector field \( 
abla f +  rac{\partial }{\partial t} \) is parallel with respect to the space-time connection. A steady gradient soliton may be considered as a space-time product where the geodesic line is tangent to the vector field \( 
abla f +  rac{\partial }{\partial t} \) . The assumption that \( R \) attains its maximum in space and time implies that the space-time Ricci tensor has a zero. On the other hand, Hamilton's matrix estimate, in the case of ancient solutions, says that the space-time Riemann curvature operator is nonnegative. Thus Theorem 15.33 may be interpreted as a space-time splitting theorem.

